/* Wealth Lab Module 14 · Rental Property and Real Estate Investing · 2026 */
window.WealthLabAcademicM14Data={"schema":"wealth-lab-m14-academic-v1","version":"3.0.0","reviewDate":"2026-11-02","taxYear":2026,"lessonIds":["L14.S1","L14.S2","L14.S3","L14.S4"],"lessons":[{"id":"L14.S1","title":"Rentals: Revenue vs Cash Flow","subtitle":"Investment-property operating statements, rent roll quality, accounting, vacancy, debt service and investor cash","prerequisites":["L1.S2 household liquidity and budgeting","L6.S1 financial statements and tax basis","L7.S3 time value of money","L13.S3 mortgage and property ownership"],"objectives":["Distinguish scheduled rent, effective gross income, NOI, net cash flow and taxable income","Construct annual rent rolls and reconcile collections with lease terms and deposits","Forecast vacancy, delinquency, concessions and seasonal lease-up costs","Classify operating costs, maintenance, reserves, capital improvements and debt service","Calculate operating breakeven and explain why positive NOI is not profit to spend","Compare gross-rent multipliers with fully adjusted property cash flows","Prepare defensible owner operating budgets, maintenance ledgers and lender exhibits"],"sections":[{"title":"A rental is an operating business before it is an investment thesis","body":["A landlord sells occupancy rights under enforceable lease terms. Revenue is created by valid rent charges and actually collected cash, while an investment owner must also operate, repair, insure, advertise, collect, inspect, account and comply with state law. Property value appreciation is not cash that pays today's mortgage. Unlike a savings account, the property can demand additional capital without notice and its market value may fall at the same time.","Start with a property operating statement for the selected time period. Gross potential rent means the contracted or market rent assuming full scheduled occupancy, before vacancy and concessions. Effective gross income subtracts realistic vacancy, nonpayment and concessions and adds documented recurring ancillary income, such as eligible parking charges. Net operating income (NOI) subtracts ordinary property operating expenses but excludes loan principal and interest, income taxes and generally capital improvements.","Capital expenditures, financing and owner-level income tax follow below the NOI line in an investor cash waterfall. Treating mortgage principal as an operating expense contaminates cap-rate comparisons, while forgetting loan principal when estimating distributable cash exaggerates how much the owner can spend. State each metric's definition before comparing properties."],"worked":["Monthly contractual rent $1,800 × 12 = $21,600 scheduled annual rent.","Forecast vacancy/credit loss $1,300 => effective gross income $20,300, assuming no other income.","Operating expenses $7,500 => NOI $12,800.","Annual debt service $14,000 => cash flow −$1,200 before capex, taxes and reserve changes."],"practice":[["Why does positive NOI not guarantee positive owner cash flow?","Debt service, major replacements, taxes, lease-up and reserves remain payable below NOI; an investor can have positive operating income but negative available cash."],["Why should an investor avoid counting property appreciation as rent cash?","Appreciation is uncertain and normally cannot pay today's invoices without a separate sale or additional borrowing."]]},{"title":"The rent roll: billed income is not a bank deposit","body":["A rent roll lists each unit, lease rate, concession, occupancy dates, deposit and delinquency. One property may advertise $2,000 per month but give a free first month, producing a lower first-year effective rent than twelve times the headline. Lease terms also determine responsibility for water, heat, common-area utilities, trash and certain maintenance. Investor diligence must inspect actual leases and payment ledgers, not just a seller's marketing rent figure.","Suppose two apartments each lease at $1,400 a month, yielding $33,600 annual scheduled rent. If one apartment is vacant two months, modeled vacancy cost is $2,800 and effective collected rent cannot exceed $30,800 absent other income. Add $700 likely uncollected rent and the operating collection forecast becomes $30,100. This loss is not a fee line item in the same way as insurance; it is income not received.","Separate refundable tenant security deposits from owner revenue. Connecticut deposits remain tenant property, with landlord security rights and statutory escrow obligations. A $2,800 refundable deposit does not create $2,800 additional spendable income. The owner must retain the cash and account for interest and eventual refunds."],"worked":["Two $1,400/month units give $2,800×12=$33,600 annual scheduled rent.","Two months vacancy on one unit costs $2,800.","Expected delinquency $700 => effective rental receipts $30,100.","A separate $2,800 tenant security deposit is not additional rent revenue or owner equity."],"practice":[["Why is a refundable security deposit excluded from rental gross revenue in the normal case?","The owner generally owes it back subject to authorized deductions, making it a tenant liability held in custody rather than earned rent."],["What documents must verify billed versus actually received rental income?","Executed leases, rent ledger, bank deposits, concessions, arrears and occupancy records."]]},{"title":"Vacancy is a probability distribution, not only a five-percent line","body":["Analysts often assume a stabilized vacancy percentage without distinguishing physical vacancy from economic vacancy. Physical vacancy measures unavailable or unrented space; economic vacancy also reflects concessions and nonpayment. A three-month unit turnover may combine vacancy rent loss, repainting, screening, utilities and leasing commissions. Those effects occur together when the property is most cash constrained.","Take a single-family rental at $1,800 monthly rent. One empty month removes $1,800 of rent, or 8.33% of a $21,600 scheduled annual total, even before marketing and turnover costs. A model that simply reserves five percent for vacancy would include only $1,080; it would understate one full vacancy month by $720. More severe vacancies can coincide with layoffs, weak housing markets or major repairs.","Use a multi-year probability framework: base occupancy, one-month vacancy and three-month vacancy; distinguish actual turnover observations from forecasts. When comparing two neighborhoods, examine local rent evidence and competing supply, not only a projected cap rate. Never extrapolate occupancy from one unusually successful year."],"worked":["Gross potential rental income $21,600.","Five-percent conventional vacancy assumption $1,080.","One full vacant month lost rent $1,800, equal to 8.33% of scheduled rent.","Three vacant months cost $5,400 before turnover and marketing expense."],"practice":[["Why can a five-percent annual vacancy reserve fail in a one-unit rental?","A single vacant month costs 8.33% of annual gross scheduled rent, and turnover costs can compound the shortfall."],["How does economic vacancy differ from physical vacancy?","Economic vacancy includes lost collections, concessions and credit loss even when a unit is physically occupied."]]},{"title":"Operating expenses: reconcile actual costs to the right line","body":["Recurring property operating expenses commonly include insurance, municipal property taxes, repairs and maintenance, owner-paid utilities, management, advertising, landscaping, snow removal and certain administrative costs. A lender or appraiser should distinguish property-related expenses from owner income tax and principal payments. Selling expenses, additions and reroofing projects are generally not routine NOI operating expenses, though economic budgeting should set cash aside for future replacements.","Consider effective gross revenue $30,100, annual real-estate tax $5,400, property insurance $1,500, normal repairs $2,400, management $2,400 and owner utilities $1,200. Operating costs total $12,900 and NOI is $17,200. Mortgage debt service is then deducted in the owner's financing layer. A reserve contribution for roof replacement may be modeled separately even if an accountant treats the actual roof as a capitalized improvement.","Do not confuse property income tax deductions with NOI. Federal Schedule E may deduct mortgage interest and depreciation under its own rules even when principal is a real cash outflow; an annual tax loss is possible despite positive investor cash flow and vice versa. A proper dashboard shows operating, financing, capital and taxable-income views side by side."],"worked":["Effective gross income $30,100.","Taxes $5,400 + insurance $1,500 + repairs $2,400 + management $2,400 + utilities $1,200 = $12,900.","NOI $30,100−$12,900=$17,200.","With $15,000 annual debt service, preliminary financing cash flow $2,200 before replacements and owner tax."],"practice":[["Why is principal repayment excluded from standard NOI?","NOI is calculated before financing so property operations can be compared independently of an investor's debt structure."],["How can property be cash flow positive but show a federal taxable loss?","Tax depreciation and mortgage interest may exceed taxable rent after deductions, while some real cash outflows like principal are not deductions."]]},{"title":"A complete cash waterfall distinguishes accounting and liquidity","body":["Investment cash flow proceeds in defined stages: gross scheduled income; less vacancy and credit loss; plus applicable ancillary revenues; less operating expenses to NOI; less required mortgage payments to cash after debt; less replacements, upfront leasing and owner cash obligations to distributable cash. Refinancing proceeds or a one-time property sale are not operating rent earnings. Rent money collected before bills are due is not distributable profit.","Example: NOI $17,200, annual debt service $15,000, planned annual replacement reserve $2,000, and a one-time $4,000 renovation. Cash after debt is $2,200, after planned reserve only $200 and after renovation −$3,800. The owner needs an additional source of liquidity. Loan principal amortization may build equity but it cannot automatically finance that same renovation.","Use separate bank accounts, a monthly bank reconciliation and an approved reserve-release policy. A property can be solvent on paper yet temporarily unable to pay a deductible. If the household is building its primary-home down payment, rental cash needs must not be silently funded from protected housing savings."],"worked":["NOI $17,200 less debt service $15,000 => cash after debt $2,200.","Less reserve allocation $2,000 => distributable operating cash $200, if no other obligations.","Less a $4,000 current renovation => year's net free cash −$3,800.","Account for any lender escrow and differences between reserve allocations and actual cash payments."],"practice":[["Why does a reserve allocation reduce owner's distributable cash even if it is not an immediate contractor invoice?","Reserves earmark money against expected future replacements, preventing overdistribution of cash needed to operate the asset."],["Can mortgage principal pay a renovation because it increases equity?","Not without a separate cash source or new approved borrowing; amortization is an economic equity increase rather than liquid money."]]},{"title":"Cash breakeven, rent sensitivity and fixed-cost operating leverage","body":["A property with high fixed costs is sensitive to relatively small changes in realized rent. If annual owner-required payments and expenses total $22,000 while projected rent collections are $24,000, a $2,000 cushion can disappear with one vacant month or a higher insurance renewal. Cash breakeven is the effective rent required to fund all defined cash obligations, not merely annual interest or a cap-rate target.","Suppose annual operating expenses $7,500, debt payments $14,000 and necessary capital reserve $1,500, with no ancillary revenue. Cash breakeven effective collections are $23,000. If vacancy is expected to absorb 6% of scheduled rent, required annual contracted gross rent is $23,000/(1−.06)≈$24,468, or $2,039 per month. This formula assumes the operating expense inputs do not increase as rents rise, which may be unrealistic for management costs.","An investor should compute break-even occupancy and the rent cushion separately. Low initial occupancy can be a temporary issue for a new property; repeated negative cash flow suggests the financing, purchase price or rent estimate is incompatible with the household's objectives. Foreclosure risk is not eliminated by long-term expected appreciation."],"worked":["Operating expense $7,500 + annual debt $14,000 + replacement reserve $1,500 = $23,000 cash need.","With 6% annual vacancy and no other income, required gross scheduled rent $23,000/.94≈$24,468.","Required monthly scheduled rent ≈$2,039.","At rent only $1,800/month, annual scheduled $21,600 cannot meet this breakeven under stated assumptions."],"practice":[["Why is a cash-break-even rent different from NOI breakeven?","Cash breakeven also includes contractual debt payments and reserves that NOI ordinarily excludes."],["What assumption makes dividing required cash by (1−vacancy rate) potentially optimistic?","Operating expenses are held fixed, although management, utilities, advertising or maintenance might increase."]]},{"title":"Connecticut rental administration and the tenant-money ledger","body":["Connecticut statutory law limits many residential security deposits to two months' rent for a tenant under 62, or one month's rent for a tenant age 62 or older. The deposit remains tenant property and must be treated under the legally required Connecticut escrow framework. For 2026, the Connecticut Department of Banking published a 0.49% rental security deposit interest rate. A landlord cannot use the deposit as if it were available equity for a down payment or operating profit.","On a hypothetical $1,800 monthly lease, a $3,600 deposit can fall within the general two-month cap for a younger tenant but exceed the one-month cap for a tenant 62 or older. A full-year illustrative simple interest calculation on $3,600 at 0.49% produces $17.64, subject to actual accrual dates and statutory rules. Interest owed to a tenant is not an investor bonus; tenant deposits are separate custodial liabilities.","Connecticut property tax depends on municipality, assessed value and mill rates, while insurance, registration, safety and habitability rules may change. A Vernon-area investor must check current town assessor records, lease requirements, local rental rules and property conditions; no state-average property-tax quote is reliable for one specific building. Treat all applicable state and municipal obligations as a dated diligence checklist."],"worked":["Monthly rent $1,800; illustrative deposit for younger tenant $3,600 = two months' rent.","2026 CT security deposit interest 0.49%, simple annual hypothetical on $3,600 = $17.64.","For tenant 62 or older, generic one-month limit => $1,800 rather than $3,600.","Deposit principal $3,600 remains a tenant liability; omit it from NOI and owner equity."],"practice":[["Why is the deposit escrow account not free cash that improves investor return?","The deposit remains tenant property subject to statutory custody, interest and refund obligations."],["Does an investor have enough information to forecast property tax by knowing only that a rental is in Connecticut?","No. Property-specific assessment, municipality, mill rates and possible tax changes are needed."]]},{"title":"Independent underwriting: four years and four bank ledgers","body":["A bank-quality rental model starts with trailing operating statements, current lease and rent roll, bank deposit records, maintenance invoices, tax bills, insurance declarations and lender statements. Reconcile a seller's claimed NOI to actual trailing cash and identify deferred repairs. An appraisal does not substitute for independent underwriting of expense assumptions and required capital reserves.","Forecast year-one, a stabilized year, a turnover year and a stress year in both nominal and inflation-adjusted dollars. Include rent increases only where market evidence and law support them. Use real numbers for property tax and premium renewals where known; otherwise show ranges. List the source of every input, who verified it, and how a 20% cost overrun affects household liquidity.","End with a buy/renegotiate/pass recommendation based on total cash capital required, positive cash stress margin, legal rent and safety compliance, and portfolio concentration. Someone able to save only $250–$500 per month should not treat a property with $6,000 expected annual cash deficit as an independent income machine."],"worked":["Seller claims NOI $17,200; lender debt payment $15,000 => $2,200 pre-reserve cash.","Replacement reserve $2,000 lowers spendable operating cash to $200.","An additional unforeseen $4,000 heating repair creates −$3,800 net annual cash need.","A $250 monthly household savings capacity equals $3,000 yearly, insufficient for that shortfall without other funding."],"practice":[["Why are current leases and bank deposits both necessary to verify a rental's revenue?","Leases establish agreed charges; deposits verify collections and reveal concessions, arrears and payment timing."],["What makes an underwriting memo reproducible?","Cited source documents, explicit assumptions, exact formulas, separate cash/tax layers and sensitivity to realistic expenses and vacancy."]]}],"sourceRefs":[["IRS Publication 527 residential rental income, repairs and depreciation","https://www.irs.gov/publications/p527"],["IRS Schedule E reporting guidance","https://www.irs.gov/instructions/i1040se"],["Connecticut General Statutes Chapter 831 security deposits","https://www.cga.ct.gov/2026/sup/chap_831.htm"],["CT Department of Banking 2026 deposit interest rates","https://portal.ct.gov/dob/rental-security-deposits/rental-security-deposits/deposit-index-and-interest-rates"],["CT DOB escrow rights, landlord tenant deposits","https://portal.ct.gov/dob/rental-security-deposits/rental-security-deposits/rental-security-deposits"],["CFPB mortgage loan estimate and costs","https://www.consumerfinance.gov/owning-a-home/loan-estimate/"],["IRS rental real estate passive activity instructions","https://www.irs.gov/instructions/i8582"],["Connecticut Judicial Branch landlord tenant self help","https://www.jud.ct.gov/lawlib/law/landlord.htm"]],"assessment":{"title":"Rental property operating-company statement","prompt":"Prepare a lender-style rental cash waterfall for a two-unit asset with rent $1,400/month/unit, two months vacancy at one unit, $700 expected uncollectible rent, property tax $5,400, insurance $1,500, repairs $2,400, management $2,400, utilities $1,200, annual debt service $15,000, planned reserve $2,000, and one-time capital repair $4,000. Reconcile with separate one-unit $1,800/month illustration using $1,300 vacancy, $7,500 expenses and $14,000 debt. Explain tenant deposits under Connecticut's 2026 legal rules, rate 0.49%, and demonstrate 6% vacancy cash-break-even math with $23,000 annual cash need.","rubric":["Rent roll, actual collections and deposit separation (25%)","NOI, debt, capex and cash waterfall reconciliation (30%)","Vacancy sensitivity and break-even rent (20%)","Connecticut legal/ethical tenant accounting (15%)","Underwriting evidence and household liquidity judgment (10%)"],"answerKey":["Two-unit scheduled rent $33,600; loss $2,800 vacancy+$700 credit = effective $30,100.","Expenses $12,900 => NOI $17,200; debt $15,000 => $2,200; reserve $2,000 => $200; current capital work $4,000 => −$3,800 cash.","One-unit example NOI $12,800; after $14,000 debt = −$1,200 before major expenses.","Breakeven gross contracted annual rent $23,000/.94≈$24,468, monthly≈$2,039.","2026 CT refundable deposit remains tenant asset with escrow; $3,600×0.0049=$17.64 hypothetical full-year interest; general two-month cap under age 62 and one-month cap at 62+."]}},{"id":"L14.S2","title":"Cap Rate, Cash-on-Cash and Leverage","subtitle":"Valuation, debt service, financial mathematics, loan-to-value, financing sensitivity and equity return","prerequisites":["L14.S1 NOI and operating cash","L7.S3 discounted cash flows","L9.S2 bonds and interest rates","L10.S1 portfolio risk","L6.S2 mortgages and financing"],"objectives":["Derive capitalization rate and direct-cap property valuation","Compute cash-on-cash return using all acquisition cash, not only down payment","Build fixed-rate amortization, annual debt service, and principal-interest split","Calculate debt service coverage and loan-to-value and compare lender covenants","Explain positive/negative leverage and cash-on-cash versus equity IRR","Construct refinance and rate shock scenarios without treating loan proceeds as profit","Use discounted cash flow and sensitivity matrix to evaluate offer price"],"sections":[{"title":"Cap rate separates property operations from an investor's financing","body":["Capitalization rate is normalized annual NOI divided by a supported property price or value. It is an unlevered income yield proxy, not the investor's total return, loan interest rate, or a guarantee of appreciation. If stabilized NOI equals $12,000 and an asset price is $200,000, direct cap is 6%. Inverting the equation yields $12,000/0.06=$200,000 indicated value using a defended market cap rate.","A lower cap rate can reflect lower perceived risk or expected growth, but may also reflect overly aggressive buyer assumptions or temporary market dislocations. A cap rate derived from an unusually low tax bill and artificially suppressed management expenses may misprice the asset. Explicitly normalize property tax after acquisition, insurance, repairs, vacancy, tenant turnover and market management fees.","A property can have a 7% cap rate yet lose money after paying a high-interest mortgage. Conversely a low cap rate can be profitable if appreciation, amortization and rent growth occur, but those outcomes are uncertain. Report cap and leveraged cash separately rather than claiming one is the full investment return."],"worked":["Annual stabilized NOI $12,000 / $200,000 price =6% cap.","At identical NOI and 5% market cap, direct value $240,000.","At 7% cap, direct value ~$171,429, a $68,571 difference from 5% cap.","These valuations change based on NOI normalization and market participants, not bank quotes alone."],"practice":[["Is a 6% cap rate equivalent to guaranteed 6% annual investor profit?","No. It describes NOI relative to property value before debt and excludes capital costs, tax and valuation changes."],["Why can a modest change in assumed cap rate materially change a property's appraised value?","Direct capitalization divides NOI by the assumed market cap; a smaller denominator increases indicated value nonlinearly."]]},{"title":"Cash-on-cash measures distributable yield on cash actually invested","body":["Cash-on-cash return is annual before-tax owner cash flow divided by the total investor cash committed, often including down payment, loan fees, acquisition closing costs and initial capital work. The numerator must be defined consistently: some use cash after debt before reserves, others after planned capital reserves. Report both if used. Omitting closing costs makes a deal look artificially efficient.","Suppose price $200,000 with $50,000 down, $6,000 financing and closing costs, and $4,000 initial repairs, giving $60,000 total cash invested. If adjusted annual cash after debt and expected recurring replacement reserve is $3,000, cash-on-cash equals 5%. Using only the $50,000 down payment as denominator would report 6%, overstating the comprehensive investment-cash yield.","Cash-on-cash is not annualized total return because loan principal amortization changes equity and market value can rise or fall. It ignores the timing of capital injections, refinancing and exit; for multi-year comparisons, calculate equity internal rate of return or present value on dated cash flows. A negative first-year cash-on-cash does not mathematically prove long-run loss, but creates a real funding need."],"worked":["Down payment $50,000+closing/financing costs $6,000+initial repairs $4,000 = $60,000 cash invested.","Annual cash after debt and recurring replacement reserve $3,000.","Cash-on-cash on true acquisition cash = $3,000/$60,000=5%.","Misleading down-payment-only calculation $3,000/$50,000=6%."],"practice":[["Why must closing costs and initial repairs be included in invested-cash denominator?","They are actual investor cash required to make the property operational; excluding them inflates yield."],["Is a 5% cash-on-cash figure comparable to the long-term IRR of a stock investment without adjustments?","No. Cash-on-cash is a one-period distribution measure, whereas IRR incorporates cash timing, equity value, capital calls and sale proceeds."]]},{"title":"Mortgage payment mathematics and amortization equity","body":["Property financing begins with loan principal, note rate, term and payment frequency. For a level-payment fully amortizing loan, monthly payment M = P r/(1−(1+r)^−N), where P is principal, r is monthly rate and N is number of payments. At r=0, the limit is P/N. Actual financing may include adjustable rates, balloon maturity, escrow, interest-only periods, origination costs and prepayment charges.","For an illustrative $150,000 fully amortizing 30-year note at 7% nominal annual interest, r=.07/12 and N=360, producing a monthly principal-and-interest payment of about $998. Annual debt service is roughly $11,977 excluding taxes, insurance and other costs. First month interest is $875, so only about $123 reduces principal. Subsequent principal grows as the balance declines; the property owner owes the full payment even when rent is late.","A loan's amortization can build owner equity, but the principal portion is not tax-deductible interest and is not spendable monthly cash. Compare interest cost, down payment, total payment, required debt-service reserves and loan-to-value before recommending leverage. Debt expenses should not be double-counted if tax and insurance are already in operating expenses."],"worked":["P=$150,000, monthly rate .07/12, term 360 payments.","Monthly P&I ≈$997.95.","First-month interest $150,000×.07/12=$875; principal component ≈$122.95.","Debt service annual ≈$11,975, excluding escrow and unusual fees."],"practice":[["Why is monthly principal repayment different from an income-tax deduction for interest?","Principal reduces outstanding liability, while deductible interest, subject to rules, is the financing cost paid to lender."],["What common double count inflates projected operating expenses?","Adding mortgage escrow for property tax and insurance when those same charges are already included directly in NOI operating expenses."]]},{"title":"Debt-service coverage and loan-to-value: two independent lender tests","body":["Debt service coverage ratio (DSCR) equals stabilized property NOI divided by contractual annual loan debt service. A DSCR of 1.20 means the property produces $1.20 NOI for each $1.00 of required debt payment before capex and taxes. Lenders' actual qualifying NOI definitions, reserve requirements and DSCR covenants differ; a borrower cannot assume a generic 1.20 minimum is a universal bank rule.","Loan-to-value (LTV) is outstanding loan balance divided by property market value. A $150,000 loan against a $200,000 property equals 75% LTV. LTV assesses collateral leverage rather than income sufficiency. A property can have 50% LTV but weak DSCR if it is vacant, or high LTV but strong current DSCR where rent is robust. Both can worsen under price and rental shocks.","Suppose NOI $12,000 and annual debt service $10,000; DSCR=1.20. If NOI declines to $8,000 during vacancy, DSCR becomes 0.80, signaling operating income insufficient to cover debt before reserves. Even if lender covenants permit temporary exceptions, household cash must pay the shortfall. Stress both income and collateral independently."],"worked":["NOI $12,000 / annual debt $10,000 ⇒ DSCR 1.20.","Loan $150,000 / value $200,000 ⇒ LTV 75%.","After NOI shock to $8,000, DSCR becomes 0.80.","A 20% property value drop to $160,000 with unchanged $150k principal raises LTV to 93.75%."],"practice":[["Does DSCR 1.20 prove a property has enough cash for a roof replacement?","No. DSCR is calculated from NOI and debt payments; capital replacement costs and owner tax can be excluded."],["Can favorable LTV make up for missing monthly debt payments automatically?","No. Equity is not cash until financing or sale occurs, both constrained by market conditions and lender requirements."]]},{"title":"Leverage amplifies gains and losses on owner equity","body":["Leverage increases the value of assets controlled relative to investor equity. If a property worth $200,000 is financed with a $150,000 loan, initial equity before costs is $50,000. A 10% property-price rise to $220,000 adds $20,000 to gross equity, about 40% of initial equity, ignoring financing costs, tax and operating cash. A 10% price fall to $180,000 cuts equity to $30,000, a 40% gross equity loss.","These equity changes are not equivalent to annualized investor IRR and are not spendable until capital can be extracted. Loan balances can decline over time, but selling costs and transaction taxes reduce realized proceeds. Negative leverage can occur when the property's unlevered after-cost return is less than debt financing cost, undermining cash flow despite positive rent.","Owners sometimes refinance after appreciation and count cash taken out as 'profit'. It is usually new debt, with future payments and risks, not earned income. A real economic analysis pairs cash-out proceeds with the resulting liability and recalculates DSCR and refinancing costs. A lender can refuse refinancing when values or rents weaken."],"worked":["Property $200,000, debt $150,000 ⇒ initial gross equity $50,000.","10% price gain => $220,000−$150,000=$70,000 equity, +40% on starting equity.","10% price loss => $180,000−$150,000=$30,000 equity, −40% on starting equity.","A sale at $180,000 with 6% selling costs leaves only $19,200 before taxes and other charges, far below original $50,000."],"practice":[["Why does debt leverage magnify percent changes in equity?","The property price changes on total asset value while debt is fixed in the short term, so a larger share of changes falls on a smaller equity base."],["Does cash-out refinancing create an investment profit equal to the borrowed cash?","No. The cash comes with a corresponding liability, interest expense and new default risk."]]},{"title":"Borrowing rate and lender covenant sensitivity","body":["Borrowing cost can erase operating cash flow even without a rent decrease. At higher interest rates, level-payment mortgages require larger monthly payments for the same principal and term. A borrower using a 5% quote on a deal that ultimately closes at 8% can overstate annual cash flow by thousands of dollars. Underwrite the signed note terms and model rate reset where the loan is adjustable.","Illustrative $150,000 principal amortized over 30 years costs about $805 monthly at 5% versus approximately $1,101 at 8%. That ~$296 monthly difference is about $3,550 annually. If an initial $2,500 annual cash surplus existed at 5%, the higher payment can turn it negative even with identical NOI. Property taxes and insurance could rise at the same time.","Loans also contain escrow requirements, recourse provisions, maturity/balloons, prepayment penalties and due-on-sale clauses. Investor assumptions must match the actual product offered; investment-property borrowing often differs from owner-occupied financing. Do not imply that first-time homebuyer assistance intended for a primary residence automatically finances a standalone rental property."],"worked":["30-year $150,000 mortgage at 5% monthly P&I approximately $805.","Same loan at 8% approximately $1,101.","Monthly increase approximately $296; yearly debt cash need increases roughly $3,550.","A $2,500 annual initial surplus under 5% may become ≈−$1,050 under 8%."],"practice":[["Why should an underwriting spreadsheet vary loan interest rates?","Interest affects contractual payments and DSCR; a deal only positive at one favorable quote may fail at closing or adjustment."],["Can an investor use primary-residence mortgage assistance on any rental property automatically?","No. Owner occupancy and program restrictions must be verified; rental-only property financing can differ materially."]]},{"title":"Direct capitalization versus discounted property cash flow","body":["Direct capitalization estimates asset value by dividing a stabilized single-year NOI by a market cap rate. Discounted cash flow (DCF) models every future property's operating cash, major capital expenses and a terminal sale. An equity DCF includes loan payments and net sale after mortgage payoff; an unlevered asset DCF excludes financing and applies an appropriate asset discount rate. Mixing cash definitions with an unrelated discount rate produces inconsistent values.","To value a simplified perpetuity with constant annual NOI $12,000 at an 8% capitalization rate, the direct-cap price would be $150,000. But a property with expected growing NOI, a roof replacement in year 3 and costly exit at year 5 cannot safely be modeled by one perpetuity formula. Its DCF requires dated annual income, capital calls, sale proceeds, expenses and discounting.","For a two-year illustrative equity model, invest $60,000 now, receive $3,000 at year one, and at year two receive $3,000 plus $67,000 net sale proceeds. At a hypothetical 10% required equity return, NPV = −$60,000+$3,000/1.10+$70,000/1.10²≈$579. This narrow scenario excludes taxes and return uncertainty; changing the sale price can reverse the result."],"worked":["Simple direct cap value NOI $12,000/.08=$150,000.","Illustrative equity dated cash flows −$60,000 now, +$3,000 year 1, +$70,000 year 2 including sale.","NPV at 10%=−$60,000+$3,000/1.10+$70,000/1.10²≈+$579.","Value is contingent on actual final net proceeds, not a forecasted gross sale price."],"practice":[["Why should an unlevered NOI capitalization not use the investor's after-debt cash flow as numerator?","Doing so mixes property-level return with owner-specific financing, making market cap comparisons inconsistent."],["Why can a DCF add value beyond a stabilized cap-rate estimate?","It models varying annual rents, expenses, capex, financing and sale timing instead of assuming one representative perpetual year."]]},{"title":"A committee-ready buy/renegotiate/pass scorecard","body":["An investment committee should use separately labeled property value, all-cash acquisition requirement, initial yield, stressed annual cash, DSCR, LTV, equity at risk, capital reserve and expected exit. Include comparable recent local sales and rents with source dates but avoid assuming comparable buildings are identical. State how a roof age, tenancy problem or tax reassessment changes the underwriting and valuation.","Compare two illustrative $200,000 properties. Property A has NOI $12,000, cap 6%, and annual debt service $10,000 for DSCR 1.20. Property B has NOI $14,000, cap 7%, but annual debt $15,000 for DSCR 0.93, and negative after-debt cash before replacements. B's higher cap rate does not make it automatically safer or financeable.","Document who will manage tenant calls, the investor's time cost, financing eligibility, property tax/insurance quotes, expected time to sell and diversification. For someone still building a primary home and with a modest monthly savings surplus, the appropriate recommendation can be to delay leveraged rental investing while building capital and maintaining credit."],"worked":["Property A NOI $12,000/$200,000=6% cap; DSCR $12k/$10k=1.20.","Property B NOI $14,000/$200,000=7% cap; DSCR $14k/$15k≈0.93.","B preliminary after-debt cash −$1,000 before capex and taxes.","Report both candidates' total upfront cash and emergency reserves rather than ranking on cap alone."],"practice":[["Can a 7% cap asset be a weaker choice than a 6% cap asset?","Yes. Funding structure, deferred maintenance, tenant risk, location and negative debt coverage may dominate the apparent higher unlevered yield."],["What should justify a renegotiated purchase price?","Evidence-backed rent and cost forecasts, comparable valuation, financing limits, necessary immediate repairs and a defined minimum stress margin."]]}],"sourceRefs":[["CFPB loan estimate and mortgage borrowing disclosure","https://www.consumerfinance.gov/owning-a-home/loan-estimate/"],["CFPB mortgage payment principal and interest explanation","https://www.consumerfinance.gov/consumer-tools/mortgages/answers/key-terms/"],["IRS Publication 527 property operations and rental expenses","https://www.irs.gov/publications/p527"],["IRS Publication 544 sale cash versus gain","https://www.irs.gov/publications/p544"],["Federal Reserve economic data interest rates","https://fred.stlouisfed.org/"],["FHFA house price index methodological reports","https://www.fhfa.gov/data/hpi"],["Investor.gov diversification and investment risk","https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/real-estate-investment-trusts-reits"],["Connecticut property taxation overview","https://portal.ct.gov/opm/igpp/municipal-data/municipal-data"]],"assessment":{"title":"Financing committee: property operations versus owner-equity returns","prompt":"Prepare a fully specified underwriting memo on $200k property, $12k NOI, $150k loan, 30 years 7% interest, cash invested $60k (including 50k down, 6k costs and 4k initial repairs). Compute cap, exact amortizing P&I and annual debt, monthly interest/principal first payment, DSCR and LTV, and 10% property price loss equity and 6% sale-cost impact. Compare with $14k NOI / $15k annual debt Property B and evaluate rate sensitivity at 5% and 8%. Build two-year hypothetical DCF with −$60k, +$3k and +$70k cash flows at 10%. Write conditions for BUY, RENEGOTIATE or PASS.","rubric":["Correct capitalization and normalizing NOI (20%)","Mortgage/payment/DSCR/LTV computations (25%)","Leveraged equity and rate shock analysis (25%)","DCF/NPV consistency and cash invested (15%)","Credit, liquidity, source verification and ethical recommendation (15%)"],"answerKey":["NOI $12k/price $200k=6% cap; $150k/$200k=75% LTV.","30-year $150k at 7% P&I≈$997.95/month, first interest $875 and principal≈$122.95, annual debt≈$11,975.","DSCR $12,000/$11,975≈1.002 before other covenants and capital reserves, not 1.20 if the actual amortized loan is used.","Initial economic equity $50k; price drop to $180k leaves $30k before selling costs, or $19,200 net after 6% sale cost and loan payoff.","B DSCR $14k/$15k≈0.933 and after-debt cash −$1k.","Two-year hypothetical NPV -$60k+$3k/1.10+$70k/1.10²≈$578.51, not guaranteed."]}},{"id":"L14.S3","title":"Repairs, Reserves and Stress Testing","subtitle":"Building systems, inspection due diligence, liquidity buffers, stress cases and risk-adjusted investment decisions","prerequisites":["L14.S1 owner cash-flow waterfall","L14.S2 DSCR and leverage","L5.S2 insurance and deductibles","L10.S3 risk scenarios"],"objectives":["Distinguish routine repairs, legally capitalized improvements and owner cash budgets","Construct an age-specific capital replacement schedule for roofs, HVAC and major systems","Compute vacancy reserves, deductible cushions and minimum operating cash","Stress property tax, insurance, loss-of-rent, financing and multi-year building-system events","Explain correlations among vacancy, values, refinancing and maintenance costs","Integrate tenant legal duties and security deposit escrow into liquidity planning","Write a property-specific operational contingency policy rather than a universal 1% rule"],"sections":[{"title":"Routine maintenance, capital improvements and tax accounting differ","body":["A rental has routine recurring repairs and larger long-lived replacements. A plumbing repair or repainting between tenants may be a current operating expense, while a new roof, HVAC replacement or substantial system upgrade can be a capital improvement for federal tax purposes under facts and applicable de minimis or maintenance safe harbors. The cash leaves the bank when paid regardless of when it is deducted or depreciated.","Property operators should track the economic forecast, booked operating cost and federal basis separately. A $9,000 roof replacement can turn a year with $3,000 cash flow after routine costs into a negative $6,000 cash-flow year even if the roof must be capitalized rather than fully deducted that year. This difference between cash flow and income tax is a core educational objective, not an accounting nuisance.","An inspection is not a guarantee that major systems will last their estimated useful lives. Building age, weather, material, prior repairs, permit history and hidden defects create a distribution of future costs. Collect contractor quotes and disclosure records, inspect water intrusion and electrical/fire safety, and flag uncertainty rather than multiplying purchase price by a single simplistic maintenance percentage."],"worked":["Base annual owner cash after normal operations and debt $3,000.","Roof replacement cash $9,000 in same period.","Owner free cash after roof −$6,000.","Tax accounting may require capitalizing $9,000 rather than a same-year full operating deduction."],"practice":[["Does capitalization mean an owner does not need cash for the roof this year?","No. The contractor requires payment now; tax basis and depreciation determine when tax deductions occur, not when cash leaves."],["Why should a repair budget be informed by physical inspection instead of only a purchase-price percentage?","System ages, defects, climate and maintenance history can create large property-specific replacement costs that averages hide."]]},{"title":"Build an age-specific replacement calendar","body":["Classify each building system by observed condition, replacement probability, estimated cost and horizon. A roof with five years of plausible remaining life and $15,000 replacement cost implies a simple annual funding target of $3,000 before inflation and investment return; that is a planning amount, not a physical depreciation schedule. Separately list furnace, hot-water system, drainage, siding, appliances, driveway, and required code upgrades.","Suppose roof $15,000 in five years, HVAC $8,000 in eight, and water heater $2,000 in five. Straight-line cash funding approximates $3,000+$1,000+$400=$4,400 annually. The amounts can overlap, so actual liquidity must withstand multiple simultaneous events. An owner starting with zero cash and following this target will not yet have $15,000 in the first years if the roof fails unexpectedly.","A reserve policy should specify minimum immediate liquid balance as well as monthly sinking-fund deposits. It should avoid counting refundable deposits, borrowed credit card limits or hypothetical refinance proceeds as reserve assets. If required funding overwhelms projected rent surplus, the property may be overpriced or the owner may be undercapitalized."],"worked":["Roof $15,000/5 years=$3,000 annual target.","HVAC $8,000/8 years=$1,000 annual target.","Heater $2,000/5 years=$400 annual target.","Combined straight-line annual replacement reserve $4,400 before inflation or uncertainty."],"practice":[["Can a $4,400 annual reserve accrual guarantee all the systems can be replaced on schedule?","No. Timing uncertainty, inflation and overlapping failures require initial liquidity and scenario stress beyond arithmetic averages."],["Why should a reserve model have both replacement funding and an immediately accessible cash floor?","Savings plans accumulate over time, but an expensive component can fail before the sinking fund reaches target."]]},{"title":"Vacancy plus turnover: correlated shocks","body":["Vacancy is not an isolated subtraction from annual rent. An empty unit can simultaneously require advertising, paint, cleaning, security work, utility carrying costs and screening. Vacancy can also be correlated with declining local wages or new rental supply, which may force rent concessions and reduce the property's resale value at exactly the wrong time.","Take a rental scheduled for $1,800/month. A two-month vacancy creates $3,600 lost rent. Add $1,200 in turnover repairs, $400 marketing and $300 owner utilities: total cash impact $5,500 compared with normal occupancy. If base free cash was $3,000 a year, the property produces negative $2,500 before any additional building failures. A model that simply applies a five-percent vacancy expense will conceal much of this loss.","Use event-driven reserve math: annual fixed obligations, months of no rent, turnover contract costs and replacement horizon. A single-family rental's cash-flow variability can exceed that of a diversified 20-unit building because one vacant dwelling means 100% of scheduled occupancy is lost temporarily. This is financial concentration risk, not simply an inconvenience."],"worked":["Rent $1,800×two vacant months=$3,600 lost rent.","Turnover repair $1,200 + marketing $400 + utility $300=$1,900.","Total gross cash impact $5,500.","Base annual free cash $3,000 declines to −$2,500."],"practice":[["Why can a vacancy create an expense shock on top of lost rental income?","Turnover frequently requires cleaning, repairs, advertising, security, utilities and concessions while cash receipts stop."],["Why is a one-unit property's vacancy scenario particularly concentrated?","A single tenant departure can temporarily remove all rent revenue while most fixed property expenses continue."]]},{"title":"Inflation and property tax reset stress","body":["A rental is exposed to price increases in insurance, contractors, supplies, municipal tax and energy. Lease increases may lag these costs or be limited by tenant ability to pay and legal requirements. Property taxes should be modeled using the specific municipality's assessment, mill rate, reassessment cycle and special districts, not an unverified statewide average.","Suppose property tax is $5,400 and rises 20%, adding $1,080 annual expense. If insurance of $1,500 rises 30%, it adds $450. These two increases subtract $1,530 from NOI, independent of whether rent increases. A $2,000 annual pre-reserve surplus falls to $470 before other changes. If the owner also faces $2,000 of added maintenance, the year's cash turns negative.","Research from Connecticut tax bills and actual insurer quotes should be archived with dates. An analyst can estimate future increases for decision-making but must not present them as official rates. Premiums can change after a claim or availability restriction; a new owner should obtain insurability confirmation before becoming contractually committed to closing."],"worked":["Starting property tax $5,400×20%=$1,080 additional cost.","Insurance $1,500×30%=$450 additional cost.","NOI declines $1,530 if rent unchanged.","$2,000 original cash surplus becomes $470 before any other stress."],"practice":[["Why is an investor's assumed rent growth not automatically a hedge against property tax increases?","Rents depend on tenant demand, leases and regulations, while taxes can change independently and faster than rent can rise."],["What source should replace a generic Connecticut mill-rate estimate?","The actual property assessment and current municipality's tax record and levy, verified for possible reassessment after sale."]]},{"title":"Emergency reserve engineering from obligations","body":["Reserve sizing should reflect the longest plausible cash gap, building-system condition, lender obligations, insurance deductible and household's own emergency needs. A basic liquidity floor might cover three to six months of fixed property operating costs and required debt service, plus a separate allowance for credible emergency repairs. There is no universally correct months-of-expenses rule across tenant concentration and building types.","Suppose a one-unit rental costs $1,200/month in mortgage, insurance, tax and unavoidable operating bills regardless of occupancy, and the owner wants a five-month fixed-cost reserve. Base liquidity floor is $6,000. If an insured loss could require a $2,500 deductible, a separate $2,500 cushion brings the illustrative reserve target to $8,500. This is before considering an uninsurable $7,000 urgent repair or loss of rent.","The investor must not claim that tenant security deposit principal is available to fund this emergency; Connecticut imposes custodial and interest responsibilities, and the deposit belongs to the tenant. A household whose available investing cash is only $3,000 should report $5,500 of unmet reserve capacity against the simplified $8,500 target rather than hiding it."],"worked":["Monthly unavoidable costs $1,200; five-month funding $6,000.","Insurance deductible buffer $2,500 => suggested reserve floor $8,500.","Accessible investor reserve $3,000 leaves $5,500 unfunded.","Additional uninsured $7,000 repair might require an even larger contingency."],"practice":[["Why does holding an insurance policy not fully eliminate the cash need from fire or water losses?","Deductibles, coverage limitations, claim delays, exclusions and displacement costs can remain owner responsibilities."],["Should escrowed tenant security deposits be credited against owner capital-reserve target?","No. They are custodial tenant assets, not free equity or an operating reserve."]]},{"title":"DSCR and cash-flow stress are different warning systems","body":["Debt service coverage ratio is NOI divided by required annual debt payments. It is a property-level debt sufficiency diagnostic, not a final investor cash-flow measure. Stressing NOI for vacancies and operating costs can reduce DSCR below an underwritten covenant even when the investor has cash available. Capital expenditure checks can also empty bank accounts while reported DSCR remains above a lender minimum.","Suppose base NOI is $18,000 and annual debt service $14,000, giving DSCR about 1.29. A $5,500 economic vacancy/turnover shock and $1,530 tax/insurance increase reduce economic NOI to $10,970 if all those items meet the stated NOI definition; stressed DSCR ≈0.78. An $8,000 additional roof expense can further reduce owner liquidity but may be treated as capital rather than NOI operating expense.","Borrowing capacity is not always stable during a downturn: lender appraisal values, required DSCR, personal debt-to-income, credit scores and interest rates can change. Cash-out refinancing or a HELOC should not be the only planned emergency response. Model at least a two-year recovery where returns do not snap back after one bad year."],"worked":["Base $18,000 NOI/$14,000 annual debt =1.286 DSCR.","Vacancy/turnover economic effect $5,500 plus tax/insurance $1,530 => $10,970 stressed NOI.","Stressed DSCR $10,970/$14,000≈0.784.","An additional capital roof $8,000 affects free cash even if outside reported NOI."],"practice":[["Can a property with acceptable DSCR still have a cash crisis from a roof replacement?","Yes. Lender DSCR normally excludes major capital replacements; investor cash reserves must cover them."],["Why should a recovery scenario not assume refinancing will always solve distress?","A weak operating history or falling collateral value can undermine loan approval or increase loan cost when cash is scarce."]]},{"title":"Legal, casualty and tenant harm exposures","body":["A rental investor carries duties to maintain habitable, safe housing and follow nondiscrimination, deposits, eviction and notice procedures. Noncompliance creates financial, legal and human harm. Compliance is a distinct risk category from ordinary financial variance. Before purchase, inspect fire and carbon-monoxide requirements, electrical/structural conditions, lead paint where relevant, local permits and the property's actual insurance coverage.","Casualty insurance transfers certain covered property losses but can exclude flooding, sewer backup, certain vacancy-related losses and other perils. Property owners should model lost rent during displacement, insurer claim delays and whether flood coverage is required or prudent. An umbrella policy, entity structure and adequate liability insurance can play different roles, but no LLC automatically shields personal wrongdoing or eliminates all personal loan guarantees.","Tenant security deposits and escrow compliance are not investor opportunities. The 2026 Connecticut rental deposit interest rate is 0.49%, with statutory maximum deposits dependent on tenant age. Money that is legally held for tenants should be reconciled separately, and statutory interest and return timelines must be handled in the actual books."],"worked":["Hypothetical deposit $2,000 and 2026 CT nominal annual required rate 0.49%.","Simple full-year illustrative interest $2,000×.0049=$9.80.","Property cash reserve available from refundable tenant deposit: $0.","An emergency claim of $7,000 with $2,500 deductible leaves potential owner deductible $2,500, subject to actual coverage."],"practice":[["Why is creating an LLC insufficient by itself as a complete rental liability policy?","Insurance, legal compliance, personal guarantees and personally wrongful conduct still require independent protection and obligations."],["Does holding a tenant deposit reduce the landlord's independent need for reserves?","No. Deposits generally remain tenant property, and statutory interest and return duties restrict their use."]]},{"title":"Stress-test investment committee and operational action plan","body":["Create base, mild stress, severe stress and disaster scenarios for the same asset. At minimum vary vacancy months, collected rent, property tax, insurance, maintenance, mortgage rate if adjustable, emergency capex, resale value and available household support cash. Use coherent event timing so the model does not simultaneously assume a tenant leaves and every vacant month is re-rented instantly. Record each input's evidence and uncertainty.","Suppose a property begins with annual cash surplus $3,000. One turnover event costs $5,500 and a property tax/insurance spike adds $1,530, leaving −$4,030. A $9,000 roof failure in the same year creates total cash requirement −$13,030 before tax, potentially more than four years of the original expected surplus. The decision to invest must reflect this tail exposure, not just the initial surplus percentage.","An operational plan specifies a minimum reserve, authorized repairs, contractor contacts, insurance claim procedure, tenant communications, rent collection follow-up, lender notifications and when to stop owner distributions. A recommendation to delay purchase is analytically valid if required cash reserves exceed affordable capital after closing. The goal is to own a durable business, not merely to display a positive yield cell."],"worked":["Base annual free cash +$3,000.","Two-month vacancy/turnover −$5,500; tax/insurance spike −$1,530 => −$4,030.","Roof replacement −$9,000 => full-year cash −$13,030.","With household savings capacity $250/month, $3,000 annual savings would not cover this stress year."],"practice":[["Why can a modest positive projected cash flow be misleading for a capital-constrained buyer?","One correlated vacancy and repair event can demand years of forecast surplus at once, forcing high-cost borrowing or default."],["What belongs in a genuine contingency plan?","Cash threshold, stress triggers, repair authorization, insurance and tenant obligations, lender communication and a plan to preserve essential household money."]]}],"sourceRefs":[["IRS Publication 527 repair-versus-capital-improvement tax distinctions","https://www.irs.gov/publications/p527"],["IRS capitalization and tangible property regulations","https://www.irs.gov/businesses/small-businesses-self-employed/tangible-property-final-regulations"],["Connecticut Judicial Branch landlord tenant rules","https://www.jud.ct.gov/lawlib/law/landlord.htm"],["Connecticut statutory deposit and escrow rules","https://www.cga.ct.gov/2026/sup/chap_831.htm"],["Connecticut 2026 deposit interest rate","https://portal.ct.gov/dob/rental-security-deposits/rental-security-deposits/deposit-index-and-interest-rates"],["NFIP flood insurance general information","https://www.floodsmart.gov/"],["FEMA flood risk maps and property lookup","https://msc.fema.gov/portal/home"],["Investor.gov market and concentration risk","https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/real-estate-investment-trusts-reits"]],"assessment":{"title":"Rental downside committee: liquidity, repairs and correlated losses","prompt":"Construct a five-year reserve ledger for a property needing roof $15k in five years, HVAC $8k in eight, heater $2k in five; a $1,800 single-family monthly rent, two months vacant, $1,900 turnover, $5,400 annual municipal tax rising 20%, $1,500 insurance rising 30%, and $14,000 yearly debt service. Start from base NOI $18k and annual owner free cash $3k, then stress DSCR, roof $9k and all combined cash shocks. Calculate five months of $1,200 fixed costs plus $2,500 deductible as reserve floor versus $3k available. Explain CT deposits, public safety, casualty and financing risks. Write an emergency response policy and decision recommendation.","rubric":["Building-system replacement calendar and capex cash accounting (25%)","Vacancy/turnover and local property-cost stress (20%)","DSCR and cash stress with distinct accounting definitions (25%)","Reserve floor and tenant legal duties (15%)","Actionable operational triggers and sources (15%)"],"answerKey":["Replacement reserve targets $15k/5+$8k/8+$2k/5=$4,400 annually, before inflation and overlapping failure risk.","Two months rent loss $3,600+$1,900 turnover=$5,500.","Property tax 20% on $5,400 adds $1,080, insurance 30% on $1,500 adds $450, combined $1,530.","Base NOI $18k/debt $14k => DSCR1.286; stressed NOI $18k−$5,500−$1,530=$10,970; DSCR≈0.784 if costs classified under the chosen NOI definition.","Reserve floor five×$1,200+$2,500=$8,500, less accessible $3k=$5,500 short.","Base free cash $3k−$5,500−$1,530−$9k roof = −$13,030 worst-year cash under illustrative assumptions."]}},{"id":"L14.S4","title":"Selling, Exchanges and Property Taxes","subtitle":"Rental basis, 27.5-year building depreciation, passive losses, 1031 exchange timing, capital gains and after-tax exit decisions","prerequisites":["L11.S3 adjusted basis and capital gains","L11.S4 tax loss limitations and state taxes","L14.S1 cash versus taxable income","L14.S2 valuation and loan equity","L14.S3 repairs versus improvements"],"objectives":["Compute separated land and building depreciation with documented placed-in-service dates","Distinguish federal Schedule E taxable income from rental cash flow","Classify rental repairs, capitalized improvements and basis reductions","Apply passive-loss special allowance thresholds and other eligibility caveats","Compute pre-tax sale proceeds and adjusted-basis gain independently","Explain unrecaptured Section 1250 gain versus ordinary Section 1250 recapture","Evaluate strict 1031 exchange windows, intermediary, boot, and basis carryover","Integrate municipal conveyance costs, financing, capital gains and estate planning into hold/sell decision"],"sections":[{"title":"Cash proceeds and recognized tax gain are different ledgers","body":["A rental sale has at least three distinct questions: how much cash the owner receives after mortgage payoff and transaction costs, how much gain or loss is recognized for federal and state income tax, and what economic return results on the original and subsequent capital investments. These may differ drastically. Debt payoff reduces sale cash but is generally not a reduction in the seller's amount realized for computing capital gain.","Suppose a rental sells for $300,000, with $18,000 of selling costs and $170,000 loan payoff. Before taxes and other liens the cash proceeds are $112,000. If adjusted tax basis were $190,000, simplified gain could be $300,000−$18,000−$190,000=$92,000. The $112,000 cash and $92,000 gain are not interchangeable: one is a financing/closing result, the other is a tax-basis result.","A property's owner should gather original closing statement, land/building allocation, capital improvements, allowed or allowable depreciation, partial dispositions, refinancing records, selling expenses and prior suspended losses. Sale amount alone cannot prove taxable gain. Do not promise a 'tax-free sale' or assume all mortgage proceeds represent profit."],"worked":["Sale price $300,000−selling costs $18,000−loan payoff $170,000=$112,000 cash before tax.","Tax adjusted basis stipulated $190,000.","Amount realized $282,000, gain $282,000−$190,000=$92,000.","The $170,000 debt payoff affects cash, generally not amount realized net of selling costs."],"practice":[["Why is sale cash not identical to taxable gain?","Cash subtracts loan payoff; taxable gain uses amount realized less adjusted tax basis, incorporating depreciation and qualifying capital additions."],["Does a $170,000 outstanding mortgage automatically reduce gain on sale by $170,000?","No. Paying the secured loan reduces investor net cash but is generally not a deduction in the gain calculation."]]},{"title":"Land is not depreciable; residential rental building generally uses 27.5 years","body":["Federal tax generally permits depreciation of the qualifying residential rental building, not the underlying land. For many US residential rentals placed into service under normal General Depreciation System rules, the building is depreciated over 27.5 years using the straight-line method and a mid-month convention. First and last tax years are prorated under the applicable tables; simply dividing by 27.5 is a full-year approximation, not necessarily the exact first-year deduction.","Suppose purchase price is $250,000 with defensible land allocation $50,000 and eligible depreciable building basis $200,000. Illustrative full-year building depreciation is $200,000/27.5≈$7,272.73. The date available for rent—not the date of first rental deposit—can determine placed-in-service date. Properly capitalized improvements may begin separate depreciation schedules under their own recovery treatment.","Property converted from personal to rental use has special basis rules involving adjusted basis and fair market value at conversion. An investor may not depreciate an arbitrary high advertised building value merely because nearby sales rose. Depreciation allowed or allowable generally reduces adjusted basis and raises recognized gain on later sale; refusing to claim a deduction does not necessarily preserve basis."],"worked":["Purchase $250k; assumed land $50k, building $200k.","Land full-year depreciation $0; building full-year straight-line approximation $200k/27.5=$7,272.73.","Five full-year equivalent years reduce adjusted basis by ~$36,363.64 before improvements and other adjustments.","Actual first-year mid-month amount depends on placed-in-service month and IRS tables."],"practice":[["Why can a landlord not claim 27.5-year tax depreciation for land?","Land generally does not wear out on a determinable schedule for federal depreciation purposes; building basis is separately allocated."],["Does skipping eligible depreciation automatically prevent a reduction in basis at sale?","No. Depreciation allowed or allowable must be considered under applicable tax rules, so not claiming it can still cause basis/tax issues."]]},{"title":"Schedule E and passive losses: a deduction is not a cash subsidy","body":["Taxable rental profit generally starts from rental income and allowable deductions such as mortgage interest, operating expenses and depreciation, subject to recordkeeping and limitations. Mortgage principal is not an ordinary tax deduction, although it reduces cash. A positive cash result can coexist with a reported tax loss from noncash depreciation. This does not mean the government sends an automatic refund equal to the modeled loss.","Rental activities are generally passive under federal Section 469 rules unless an exception applies. The special allowance for active participation can allow up to $25,000 of qualifying rental loss against nonpassive income for eligible individual filers, but it phases out over modified AGI $100,000 to $150,000 for many single and married-joint taxpayers; special married-filing-separately limitations and other restrictions apply. A real estate professional must satisfy separate legal tests, not merely describe themselves as an investor.","Suppose qualifying active participant has rental loss $12,000 and correctly computed MAGI $120,000. Simplified special allowance ceiling = $25,000−0.5×($120,000−$100,000)=$15,000, potentially enough for the $12,000 loss subject to all other rules. At MAGI $150,000 the same special allowance ordinarily becomes zero, so an unallowed passive loss might carry forward rather than reduce wages that year."],"worked":["Hypothetical MAGI $120,000 in an eligible actively participating rental situation.","Special allowance ceiling $25,000−0.5×$20,000=$15,000.","Qualifying $12,000 rental loss may fit inside that allowance if all other eligibility requirements hold.","At $150,000 MAGI, simplified general active-participation allowance $0 before other exceptions."],"practice":[["Does a $12,000 rental paper loss always offset $12,000 of W-2 wages?","No. Passive-activity loss rules, active participation, MAGI phaseout, at-risk limits and other exceptions determine current deductibility."],["Why is real-estate-professional status not established by simply owning several rentals?","It requires specific statutory service-hour and material-participation requirements; labels and property count alone are insufficient."]]},{"title":"Repairs, improvements and building basis recordkeeping","body":["Routine maintenance often creates a current eligible rental deduction if tax rules permit, while major betterments, restorations and adaptations generally must be capitalized and depreciated over an appropriate period. Federal tangible-property regulations contain safe harbors and facts-sensitive tests; a roof replacement, new HVAC or significant addition is not automatically the same as a deductible repair. The accounting choice influences current taxable income and future adjusted tax basis.","Suppose property originally cost $250,000 including $50,000 land and owners add a qualifying $20,000 building improvement. Before accumulated depreciation, total adjusted basis might become $270,000, while land remains $50,000 and the building/improvement basis comprises the rest. Later, accumulated depreciation reduces recognized basis. An operating spreadsheet might treat the $20,000 as a capital cash outflow in the current year, even though tax expense is spread over years.","Keep dated invoices, work orders, before/after photographs and consistent cost allocation. Estimating future maintenance reserves is economic prudence, not the same as deducting a reserve contribution on Schedule E. Avoid assuming moving money from checking to a 'roof reserve' creates a tax deduction; the tax event generally depends on actual qualifying expense and capitalization rules."],"worked":["Purchase adjusted initial basis $250,000, qualifying improvement capitalized $20,000.","Before depreciation, adjusted basis $270,000 under simplifying facts.","After $36,363.64 total allowed/allowable depreciation, illustrative basis $233,636.36.","Depositing $5,000 into a replacement reserve is normally a bank transfer, not a $5,000 federal tax deduction."],"practice":[["Why does a capital-improvement fund transfer not automatically reduce federal taxable rental income?","Saving cash into a reserve does not itself satisfy tax rules for deducting an expense or depreciating a placed-in-service capital asset."],["How can a capital improvement affect taxable gain many years later?","Capitalized eligible cost increases basis, while depreciation then reduces basis; net adjusted basis changes gain at disposition."]]},{"title":"Depreciation and unrecaptured section 1250 gain","body":["Real property depreciation usually reduces adjusted basis. On sale at a gain, the portion of gain attributable to prior depreciation can enter unrecaptured Section 1250 gain calculations, potentially subject to a maximum federal rate of 25%, with actual taxation dependent on the owner's marginal rates and other factors. This is not a promise that every depreciation dollar is automatically taxed at exactly 25%, and it is not necessarily ordinary Section 1250 recapture under special accelerated-depreciation rules.","For a simplified rental with total economic recognized gain $92,000 and prior straight-line building depreciation $30,000, up to $30,000 of gain can be in the unrecaptured Section 1250 category under qualifying facts, leaving approximately $62,000 in another long-term capital-gain category. The actual Form 4797/Schedule D worksheets include ordering, gains, losses, rate limits and other property types; this separation is an educational allocation, not final federal tax due.","Connecticut income tax, net investment income tax when applicable, capital loss offsets, passive loss release and other provisions may further change sale outcomes. A refinance does not reset cost basis to appraised market value. Keep a yearly depreciation ledger even when cash flow is low so the later sale does not surprise heirs or current owners."],"worked":["Recognized total sale gain stipulated $92,000; prior allowable building depreciation $30,000.","Simplified potential unrecaptured Section 1250 slice min($92,000,$30,000)=$30,000.","Remaining gain $62,000, before other capital-gain classification adjustments.","The 25% number is maximum federal rate on the applicable unrecaptured category, not an automatic flat tax on every rental sale."],"practice":[["Does depreciation on modern residential rental buildings invariably create ordinary Section 1250 recapture?","No. Straight-line residential real property more commonly implicates unrecaptured Section 1250 gain and its specific capital-gain rate ceiling; rules differ by property and adjustments."],["Can a landlord estimate final taxes by multiplying all $92,000 gain by 25%?","No. Character, taxpayer ordinary marginal rate, capital gain bands, losses, NIIT, state taxes and other rules determine actual liabilities."]]},{"title":"A 1031 exchange defers qualifying gain; it does not erase borrowing or taxes","body":["A federal Section 1031 like-kind exchange can defer gain when qualifying investment or business real property is exchanged for qualifying real property, subject to strict rules. A personal-use residence generally does not qualify as held for investment merely because its owner wants to defer income tax. The property must be eligible, arrangements must be timely and the taxpayer cannot simply sell for cash then purchase another property at leisure.","Deferred exchanges generally require identifying replacement property in writing within 45 days and receiving it by the earlier of 180 days or the applicable tax-return deadline including extensions. Qualified intermediaries and limitations on receipt or control of proceeds are crucial. Exchanges involving non-like-kind cash or other property, often called boot, can cause partial taxable gain; debt relief and replacement debt/equity differences complicate the calculation.","An investor who plans an exchange should not treat proceeds as free money to pay credit cards or a primary-home down payment without evaluating recognized gain and exchange qualification. A poorly structured exchange can fail and leave the seller owing tax despite already committing funds to replacement property. Complete a QI and attorney/tax adviser review before the first contract closes."],"worked":["Rental property sold and relinquished on June 1, hypothetical identification period 45 days.","Replacement candidate must be properly identified by statutory deadline measured from transfer date.","Replacement closing must occur by earlier statutory 180-day deadline or qualifying return due date.","Boot and debt shifts can require recognized gain even when exchange otherwise qualifies."],"practice":[["Can someone sell a rental for cash and decide six months later to retroactively do a 1031 exchange?","No. Identification, receipt, timing and qualified exchange arrangements must comply from the relevant transfer period."],["Does completing a qualifying 1031 exchange make gain permanently disappear?","Not ordinarily. Basis carryover and future recognition can defer tax until later taxable disposition; it does not create tax-free cash."]]},{"title":"Connecticut conveyance costs and local sale assumptions","body":["Connecticut real-estate dispositions require evaluating state conveyance taxes, possible local conveyance taxes, legal recording, title, brokerage commission and lien payoffs. State and municipal rates and exemptions can vary by value band, location, transaction classification and effective date. A seller should obtain a dated closing statement rather than assume the buyer's estimated all-in cost or an online statewide percentage captures every charge.","Connecticut property taxes owed while holding the rental are operating costs; real estate conveyance taxes and realtor commissions generally occur on transfer, and income tax on gain is separate. A model that subtracts an overall six-percent selling-cost estimate must state which closing charges it includes. Avoid adding conveyance tax twice if the estimate already includes it.","For example a $300,000 rental sale with $18,000 modeled combined selling expenses and $170,000 mortgage payoff yields $112,000 before income tax. If municipal conveyance charges not included in the $18,000 estimate turn out to be $1,000, owner pre-tax cash reduces to $111,000. No tax was 'saved' by recording a trust deed or LLC label; legal structure can change reporting requirements, liability and financing."],"worked":["Sale $300,000; combined modeled selling cost $18,000; mortgage payoff $170,000.","Pre-tax sale cash $112,000.","Additional unbudgeted $1,000 closing cost => $111,000.","Need separate estimate for federal/Connecticut gain tax based on adjusted basis, unrelated to $170,000 payoff."],"practice":[["Why must a selling-cost worksheet specify whether Connecticut conveyance tax is included?","Otherwise the model can either omit a required cost or double-count it when total sale expenses already incorporate it."],["Does creating an LLC erase the state's conveyance tax or federal tax gain by default?","No. Transaction classification and relevant state/federal rules control; a legal entity is not a universal tax exemption."]]},{"title":"Hold, sell or exchange: compare cash timing after tax","body":["A property that produces current cash can still be worth selling if repairs, concentration risk or future tax costs outweigh reasonable expected return. Conversely an asset with low current cash may have genuine value from future income and loan amortization. The decision should compare risk-adjusted present values after transaction costs, not only today's tax liability. A 1031 exchange can preserve deferred gain while acquiring another property but can also increase leverage and holding risk.","Construct three cases: keep for five years with realistic rent and capital work; sell now with modeled loan payoff, selling costs and income tax; and complete a legally valid 1031 replacement property exchange with qualifying basis and matching debt/cash requirements. The exchange case is not available unless real properties qualify and deadlines are met. Base and adverse trajectories should separately show tax recognition, cash remaining for emergencies and heir succession treatment.","Individual circumstance matters. A family trying to purchase a primary home in Connecticut should not put all accessible reserves into a thinly cash-flowing rental because paper depreciation might reduce taxes. A profitable rental investment may be postponed until household emergency funds and home-purchase cash are reliably protected. This is a capital allocation recommendation, not a judgment about landlord ambition."],"worked":["Illustrative rental NOI $12,000, annual debt $11,975 and needed capital reserve $2,000.","Pre-tax owner cash after debt and reserve ≈−$1,975 despite positive $12,000 NOI.","A sale could produce $112,000 pre-tax proceeds under separate stipulated figures but require income tax.","Compare risk-adjusted hold NPV, actual after-tax sale cash and any legally eligible tax-deferred replacement; no automatic best option."],"practice":[["Why can a property with positive rent and NOI still be an unattractive hold?","Debt, deferred capital replacements, taxes, concentration and required investor cash can exceed economic return and alternative opportunities."],["Why is tax deferral not by itself sufficient reason to exchange into a new rental?","A weak replacement can destroy more wealth through price, leverage and management risk than the tax deferral temporarily preserves."]]}],"sourceRefs":[["IRS Publication 527 residential rental depreciation and repairs","https://www.irs.gov/publications/p527"],["IRS Publication 544 property sale, exchanges and adjusted basis","https://www.irs.gov/publications/p544"],["IRS Form 8824 1031 exchange eligibility and deadlines","https://www.irs.gov/instructions/i8824"],["IRS Form 8582 passive activity loss phaseout","https://www.irs.gov/instructions/i8582"],["IRS Schedule E income and deductions","https://www.irs.gov/instructions/i1040se"],["IRS Form 4797 gain character Section 1250","https://www.irs.gov/instructions/i4797"],["Connecticut DRS real estate conveyance and property transfer","https://portal.ct.gov/drs/businesses/real-estate-conveyance-tax"],["Connecticut DRS individual capital gain reporting","https://portal.ct.gov/drs/individuals/individual-income-tax-portal"],["IRS Publication 523 personal home and rental conversions","https://www.irs.gov/publications/p523"]],"assessment":{"title":"Investment property complete tax and exit memorandum","prompt":"Compute sale proceeds for $300k sale, $18k total selling costs and $170k outstanding note; separately recognized gain at adjusted basis $190k. Build acquisition basis $250k divided $50k land, $200k building, calculate full-year 27.5-year straight-line approximation, and five years of full-year equivalent depreciation. Add a capital improvement $20k and show basis consequences. For a stipulated $92k taxable gain and $30k depreciation, classify a potential unrecaptured section 1250 slice and remaining gain without pretending to compute a final tax return. Evaluate $12k rental loss with $120k modified AGI and qualifying active participation, phaseout threshold. Compare sale vs hold vs 1031 exchange with 45/180-day and qualified intermediary requirements, and report CT conveyance and local expenses.","rubric":["Adjusted basis versus sale cash; debt payoff distinction (25%)","Depreciation, capital improvements and tax character (25%)","Passive loss phaseout and limitations (20%)","1031 exchange legal/timing qualification (20%)","Connecticut sales costs and evidence-based hold/sell ethics (10%)"],"answerKey":["Sale cash before income tax $300k−$18k−$170k=$112,000.","Amount realized $282k and stipulated adjusted basis $190k imply $92k recognized gain.","$200k building basis/27.5 ≈$7,272.73 full-year equivalent; five years ≈$36,363.64; first/last-year mid-month conventions alter actual amounts.","Building improvement $20k adds basis if capitalized, with applicable depreciation reducing it over time.","Potential unrecaptured §1250 portion min($92k,$30k)=$30k under simple qualifying facts, remaining $62k another character; 25% is maximum applicable rate not automatic tax.","Qualifying active participation $25k−.5×($120k−$100k)=$15k special allowance ceiling, so illustrative $12k rental loss might fit subject to all other limits.","Exchange requires qualifying investment/business real estate, appropriate QI or equivalent rules, property identification within 45 days and receipt within 180 days or earlier return deadline."]}}],"capstone":{"title":"Module 14 — Connecticut rental acquisition and exit investment committee","intro":"A complete investment committee package, not a promotional cap-rate worksheet. Model 2026 rental and income-tax conditions with audited income statements, cash reserves, lender covenants and a five-year exit decision. Some exhibits below deliberately represent independent comparisons; do not combine unrelated properties unless the prompt directs it.","scenario":["Property A: single-unit Connecticut residential rental, asking $200,000, currently expected normalized NOI $12,000, intended 25% down and $150,000 mortgage over 30 years at hypothetical 7% nominal fixed interest. Initial closing and lender costs $6,000, repairs $4,000 and annual replacement reserves $2,000.","Property A full underwriting should calculate the actual mortgage P&I, cap rate, DSCR, LTV, cash-on-cash before and after reserves, and price decline to $180,000 with 6% selling expense.","Property B contrast: $200,000 purchase, NOI $14,000, annual $15,000 debt service; other financing details unspecified. Compare cap rate and DSCR without pretending property B's full owner cash flow is known.","Two-unit operations case: two units each $1,400 monthly; one vacant for 2 months and $700 projected credit loss; tax $5,400, insurance $1,500, repairs $2,400, management $2,400, utilities $1,200, annual debt $15,000, reserve $2,000 and one-time $4,000 capital work.","Repairs case: HVAC $8,000 due in eight years; roof $15,000 due in five; heater $2,000 in five; emergency roof in stress year $9,000.","Correlated stress: two-month single-unit vacancy at $1,800 rent, $1,900 total turnover cost, 20% rise on $5,400 property tax and 30% on $1,500 insurance. Base property free cash +$3,000 before these stresses.","Connecticut statutory deposit administration: one $1,800 monthly lease, tenant under age 62; model full two-month security deposit in CT escrow at official 2026 deposit index 0.49%, not spendable owner income.","Finance change: 30-year amortizing $150,000 note at 5% vs 8% hypothetical interest. Quantify actual monthly payment change and how it affects owner cash.","Tax case: separate rental purchased $250,000 with supported $50,000 land allocation and $200,000 residential depreciable building. Full-year straight-line illustration uses 27.5 years, first year mid-month convention separately acknowledged.","Sale case: $300,000 agreed price, $18,000 combined selling expenses, $170,000 mortgage payoff, adjusted basis independently stipulated $190,000; use distinct cash and gain math.","Passive loss exhibit: eligible active-participation taxpayer at modified AGI $120,000, loss $12,000, no conflicting losses or special limits for simplified demonstration.","Disposition and basis: $92,000 simplified gain, $30,000 attributable to prior straight-line depreciation; address federal Section 1250 character, not an invented uniform 25% tax.","Compare immediate sale, five-year hold and legally compliant 1031 exchange, accounting for 45-day property identification, 180-day/return-due-date outer limit and qualified intermediary.","Buyer has only $250–$500 realistic monthly household savings capacity for discretionary capital. No primary-home, rent or emergency funding may be double counted.","Request actual assessor taxes, insurance, inspection, rent roll, lender quote, closing costs and Connecticut deposit statutory compliance before making purchase recommendation."],"tasks":["Build verified 12-month rent roll with vacancy/concession evidence and tenant deposit liability separated from rent.","Construct two-unit scheduled rent, economic rent, normal property expenses and NOI.","Reconcile two-unit cash after debt, after replacement reserve and after separate capital repair.","Underwrite property A's cap rate and direct cap value at 5%, 6%, 7% market cap.","Compute mortgage $150k over 360 months at 7% including first-month interest, principal and annual debt service.","Calculate property A real DSCR and LTV using actual calculated debt service.","Compute property A acquisition cash including down payment, closing and repair expenses, then cash-on-cash with a stated reserve convention.","Contrast property A and B cap rates and DSCR and explain the ambiguity of B's costs.","Evaluate 10% real estate price decrease, 6% selling costs and initial gross-versus-realized equity with unchanged debt.","Quantify 5% versus 8% 30-year financing payment sensitivity and economic break-even.","Derive a cash-break-even rent for $23,000 annual cash need at 6% vacancy, reported monthly.","Construct asset-specific roof/HVAC/water heater annual reserve ledger and immediate cash floor.","Model correlated vacancy/turnover/tax/insurance plus $9,000 roof stress; show lost cash and DSCR limitations.","Compute Connecticut 2026 tenant security deposit escrow interest with 0.49%, age-dependent limits and liability treatment.","Compute $200,000 building basis full-year depreciation at 27.5 years, independent $50,000 land nondepreciability and first-year convention caveat.","Calculate passive-loss special allowance ceiling at $120,000 correctly defined MAGI and potential treatment of $12k rental loss.","Calculate $300k sale, $18k expenses, $170k loan payoff cash and recognized gain with $190k tax adjusted basis.","Separate a stipulated $92k gain into potential $30k unrecaptured Section 1250 and other long-term portion; explain why tax rate is not uniformly 25%.","Compare sale, hold and 1031 exchange; identify property qualification, QI, identification and receipt deadlines and non-like-kind boot risks.","Deliver a 1,500–2,000 word independent investment-committee recommendation with sources, bid conditions, tax limitations, Connecticut obligations, contingency triggers and final verdict."],"answers":["Two units scheduled $33,600, less $2,800 vacancy and $700 credit loss => effective $30,100.","Expenses $5,400+$1,500+$2,400+$2,400+$1,200=$12,900; NOI $17,200; after debt $2,200; after reserves $200; after $4k capital outlay −$3,800.","Property A cap rate $12,000/$200,000=6%; direct values at 5/6/7% = $240,000/$200,000/$171,428.57.","At 7%, monthly amortization $150k×(.07/12)/(1−(1+.07/12)^−360)≈$997.95 and first-month interest $875; actual DSCR near 1.00, not 1.20.","Property A LTV 75%; cash invested $50k+$6k+$4k=$60k. Cash after actual annual debt ≈$24.59 BEFORE $2k recurring capex reserves, meaning cash-on-cash near 0.041% before reserve and negative after reserve (under precise mortgage math).","Property B cap 7%; DSCR $14k/$15k≈.933, annual cash after debt −$1,000 before capex; higher cap alone does not make it safer.","10% loss on $200k drops value to $180k; debt held $150k => gross equity $30k; 6% sale expense $10.8k leaves $19.2k before tax.","5% on $150k/30y costs ~$805 monthly, 8% ~$1,101; annual difference ≈$3,550, with actual calculation preferred.","Break-even scheduled annual rent $23k/(1−.06)≈$24,468 or $2,039/month.","Replacement funding roof 15k/5=3k, HVAC 8k/8=1k, heater 2k/5=400, annual total $4,400 with timing caveats.","Two-month vacancy $3,600 and turnover $1,900 => $5,500; taxes+insurance surge $1,080+$450=$1,530; stress with roof: $3k−$5.5k−$1.53k−$9k=−$13,030.","2026 CT deposit two months $3,600 under-62 and one month $1,800 at 62+; one-year simple interest on $3,600 at 0.49%=$17.64, while deposit principal belongs to tenant.","Residential building $200k/27.5=$7,272.73 approximate annual full-year; land $50k depreciation zero; first-year mid-month convention matters.","At MAGI $120k eligible active participation ceiling 25k−0.5(120k−100k)=$15k; a $12k qualifying loss may fit subject to additional tax restrictions.","Sale cash 300k−18k−170k=$112k; amount realized 300k−18k=$282k, less basis 190k gives $92k gain.","Potential straight-line depreciation-related unrecaptured Section 1250 gain up to $30k, other gain $62k subject to character and applicable rates.","A deferred 1031 replacement needs qualifying held-for-investment/business real property, written identification by day 45, receipt within 180 days or earlier return due date, and careful QI/boot/debt analysis."],"rubric":["Documented lease-to-NOI-to-owner-cash reconciliation (20%)","Mortgage valuation, leverage and lender covenants (20%)","Capital reserves, legal compliance and correlated stress (20%)","Basis, passive loss, 1031 and exit tax analysis (25%)","Professional judgment on household liquidity and defensible assumptions (15%)"],"sources":["https://www.irs.gov/publications/p527","https://www.irs.gov/instructions/i8582","https://www.irs.gov/publications/p544","https://www.irs.gov/instructions/i8824","https://www.irs.gov/instructions/i4797","https://portal.ct.gov/dob/rental-security-deposits/rental-security-deposits/deposit-index-and-interest-rates","https://www.cga.ct.gov/2026/sup/chap_831.htm","https://www.consumerfinance.gov/owning-a-home/loan-estimate/"]}};
